1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,794
<font color="#FFFFFF">Machine learning promises to accelerate therapeutic
antibody design,</font>

2
00:00:04,874 --> 00:00:10,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">but only if models can accurately read the
sequence-to-function fitness landscape.</font>

3
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:14,251
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces FLAb, the Fitness Landscape
for Antibodies,</font>

4
00:00:14,331 --> 00:00:17,532
<font color="#FFFFFF">the largest therapeutic antibody benchmark to date.</font>

5
00:00:17,612 --> 00:00:23,129
<font color="#FFFFFF">It gathers experimental measurements for six
developability properties and uses them to</font>

6
00:00:23,209 --> 00:00:28,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">stress-test six widely used deep learning protein
models against physics-based Rosetta.</font>

7
00:00:28,805 --> 00:00:34,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline finding is sobering: no single model
correlates well with all six properties,</font>

8
00:00:34,595 --> 00:00:38,889
<font color="#FFFFFF">and performance swings sharply across datasets of
the same property,</font>

9
00:00:38,969 --> 00:00:41,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">revealing how far the field still has to go.</font>

10
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:48,875
<font color="#FFFFFF">Machine learning can only speed up antibody design
if models truly understand what makes an antibody good.</font>

11
00:00:48,955 --> 00:00:53,272
<font color="#FFFFFF">Yet the major protein fitness benchmarks, like
CAFA, TAPE, and FLIP,</font>

12
00:00:53,352 --> 00:00:57,864
<font color="#FFFFFF">either leave antibody data out entirely or include
only a sliver of it.</font>

13
00:00:57,944 --> 00:01:03,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">That leaves researchers with no principled way to
check whether a deep learning model</font>

14
00:01:03,440 --> 00:01:05,624
<font color="#FFFFFF">actually captures antibody fitness.</font>

15
00:01:05,704 --> 00:01:10,927
<font color="#FFFFFF">Today the field often falls back on weak proxies
such as native sequence recovery,</font>

16
00:01:11,007 --> 00:01:14,160
<font color="#FFFFFF">which say little about real therapeutic potential.</font>

17
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:19,789
<font color="#FFFFFF">A therapeutic antibody has to satisfy many demands
at once: it must express well,</font>

18
00:01:19,869 --> 00:01:24,658
<font color="#FFFFFF">stay stable, avoid triggering an immune response,
bind its target tightly,</font>

19
00:01:24,738 --> 00:01:29,132
<font color="#FFFFFF">resist aggregation, and steer clear of sticking to
the wrong things.</font>

20
00:01:29,212 --> 00:01:34,527
<font color="#FFFFFF">These properties often pull against each other, so
improving one can hurt another.</font>

21
00:01:34,607 --> 00:01:39,001
<font color="#FFFFFF">If a model could reliably score candidates across
all of these axes,</font>

22
00:01:39,081 --> 00:01:42,553
<font color="#FFFFFF">it could replace slow and expensive wet-lab screening.</font>

23
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:47,422
<font color="#FFFFFF">As new antibody design methods keep appearing, the
field needs one shared,</font>

24
00:01:47,502 --> 00:01:50,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">antibody-focused benchmark to judge them fairly.</font>

25
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:56,051
<font color="#FFFFFF">To fill this gap, the authors build FLAb, the
Fitness Landscape for Antibodies,</font>

26
00:01:56,131 --> 00:02:00,436
<font color="#FFFFFF">the largest therapeutic antibody design benchmark
assembled so far.</font>

27
00:02:00,516 --> 00:02:07,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">It curates seventeen mutational landscapes with
more than thirteen thousand experimental fitness measurements,</font>

28
00:02:07,716 --> 00:02:10,189
<font color="#FFFFFF">spanning six developability properties.</font>

29
00:02:10,269 --> 00:02:11,564
<font color="#FFFFFF">Using this benchmark,</font>

30
00:02:11,644 --> 00:02:18,175
<font color="#FFFFFF">they evaluate six widely adopted pretrained protein
models and compare them to physics-based Rosetta.</font>

31
00:02:18,255 --> 00:02:19,484
<font color="#FFFFFF">Just as importantly,</font>

32
00:02:19,564 --> 00:02:24,720
<font color="#FFFFFF">all of the FLAb data are released openly so the
community can keep expanding it.</font>

33
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:26,580
<font color="#FFFFFF">The core idea is simple.</font>

34
00:02:26,660 --> 00:02:30,546
<font color="#FFFFFF">Every antibody sequence, or its structure, is fed
to a model,</font>

35
00:02:30,626 --> 00:02:36,918
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the model reports a perplexity score averaged
over all residues in the heavy and light chains.</font>

36
00:02:36,998 --> 00:02:41,014
<font color="#FFFFFF">Perplexity measures how surprised the model is by
the sequence,</font>

37
00:02:41,094 --> 00:02:45,435
<font color="#FFFFFF">so a well-behaved model should be confident,
meaning low perplexity,</font>

38
00:02:45,515 --> 00:02:47,385
<font color="#FFFFFF">about high-fitness antibodies.</font>

39
00:02:47,465 --> 00:02:52,911
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors then correlate these scores against
real experimental fitness using three</font>

40
00:02:52,991 --> 00:02:55,512
<font color="#FFFFFF">coefficients: Pearson for linear trends,</font>

41
00:02:55,592 --> 00:02:59,998
<font color="#FFFFFF">Spearman for monotonic trends, and Kendall tau for
ordinal agreement.</font>

42
00:03:00,078 --> 00:03:05,394
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially, the models are used exactly as released,
with no additional fine-tuning,</font>

43
00:03:05,474 --> 00:03:09,880
<font color="#FFFFFF">across decoder-only, encoder-only, and
inverse-folding architectures.</font>

44
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:15,562
<font color="#FFFFFF">FLAb pulls together seventeen mutational landscapes
from distinct antibody families,</font>

45
00:03:15,642 --> 00:03:17,740
<font color="#FFFFFF">drawn from eight separate studies,</font>

46
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:21,712
<font color="#FFFFFF">adding up to more than thirteen thousand fitness
measurements.</font>

47
00:03:21,792 --> 00:03:28,183
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together they cover the six developability
properties that define a therapeutic antibody: expression,</font>

48
00:03:28,263 --> 00:03:33,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">thermostability, immunogenicity, aggregation,
polyreactivity, and binding affinity.</font>

49
00:03:33,580 --> 00:03:37,345
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each property is grounded in its own real
experimental unit,</font>

50
00:03:37,425 --> 00:03:42,854
<font color="#FFFFFF">from micrograms per milliliter for expression to
melting temperature for stability and</font>

51
00:03:42,934 --> 00:03:45,097
<font color="#FFFFFF">dissociation constants for binding.</font>

52
00:03:45,177 --> 00:03:52,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">This diversity is what lets FLAb test whether a
model generalizes across the many facets of antibody fitness.</font>

53
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:56,225
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline result is a reality check.</font>

54
00:03:56,305 --> 00:04:00,070
<font color="#FFFFFF">No model correlates well with all six properties,
and even for a single property,</font>

55
00:04:00,150 --> 00:04:03,915
<font color="#FFFFFF">performance swings widely from one dataset to another.</font>

56
00:04:03,995 --> 00:04:07,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">ProGen2-Small was the most frequent winner, coming
out on top for seven datasets,</font>

57
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:11,605
<font color="#FFFFFF">while ProGen2-Medium, ProGen2-OAS, ESM-IF,</font>

58
00:04:11,685 --> 00:04:15,450
<font color="#FFFFFF">and Rosetta energy tied just behind at six each.</font>

59
00:04:15,530 --> 00:04:19,295
<font color="#FFFFFF">Notably,</font>

60
00:04:19,375 --> 00:04:23,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">sequence-based models on average beat
structure-based ones across every landscape,</font>

61
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:26,985
<font color="#FFFFFF">with the biggest gap on thermostability,</font>

62
00:04:27,065 --> 00:04:30,830
<font color="#FFFFFF">suggesting that raw sequence signal still carries
most of the predictive power here.</font>

63
00:04:31,210 --> 00:04:33,544
<font color="#FFFFFF">Digging into what drives performance,</font>

64
00:04:33,624 --> 00:04:39,154
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors find that parameter count matters more
than architecture or training data.</font>

65
00:04:39,234 --> 00:04:42,090
<font color="#FFFFFF">Encoder-only AntiBERTy and decoder-only IgLM,</font>

66
00:04:42,170 --> 00:04:47,113
<font color="#FFFFFF">both trained on the same five hundred fifty eight
million antibody sequences,</font>

67
00:04:47,193 --> 00:04:48,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">behave almost identically.</font>

68
00:04:48,890 --> 00:04:55,855
<font color="#FFFFFF">Scaling ProGen2 from one hundred fifty million up
to over six billion parameters helped only two properties,</font>

69
00:04:55,935 --> 00:04:58,139
<font color="#FFFFFF">polyreactivity and thermostability.</font>

70
00:04:58,219 --> 00:05:04,140
<font color="#FFFFFF">They also uncover an evolutionary bias: several
language models rank the wild-type golimumab</font>

71
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:08,577
<font color="#FFFFFF">antibody as fitter than mutants that are actually
more thermostable,</font>

72
00:05:08,657 --> 00:05:11,904
<font color="#FFFFFF">while physics-based Rosetta gets the ranking right.</font>

73
00:05:11,984 --> 00:05:17,710
<font color="#FFFFFF">It's a reminder that evolutionary likelihood and
physical fitness are not the same thing.</font>

74
00:05:18,090 --> 00:05:21,215
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the scope and the findings.</font>

75
00:05:21,295 --> 00:05:27,233
<font color="#FFFFFF">FLAb spans thirteen thousand three hundred eighty
four fitness measurements across seventeen</font>

76
00:05:27,313 --> 00:05:30,896
<font color="#FFFFFF">mutational landscapes and six developability properties,</font>

77
00:05:30,976 --> 00:05:34,102
<font color="#FFFFFF">testing six deep learning models against Rosetta.</font>

78
00:05:34,182 --> 00:05:38,092
<font color="#FFFFFF">Intrinsic properties, the ones driven by the
antibody itself,</font>

79
00:05:38,172 --> 00:05:41,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">reach an average absolute correlation above zero
point six,</font>

80
00:05:42,031 --> 00:05:47,119
<font color="#FFFFFF">while extrinsic properties lag well behind, with
binding under zero point four,</font>

81
00:05:47,199 --> 00:05:52,286
<font color="#FFFFFF">expression under zero point four two, and
immunogenicity under zero point five.</font>

82
00:05:52,366 --> 00:05:56,800
<font color="#FFFFFF">And models are far better at telling apart mutants
within one family,</font>

83
00:05:56,880 --> 00:06:00,594
<font color="#FFFFFF">at zero point seven seven, than across different families,</font>

84
00:06:00,674 --> 00:06:02,360
<font color="#FFFFFF">at just zero point one two.</font>

85
00:06:02,740 --> 00:06:08,307
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is honest and useful: no current deep
learning model reliably predicts antibody</font>

86
00:06:08,387 --> 00:06:11,069
<font color="#FFFFFF">fitness across all developability properties,</font>

87
00:06:11,149 --> 00:06:17,330
<font color="#FFFFFF">so we are not yet at the point of trusting these
models to filter therapeutic candidates on their own.</font>

88
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:22,241
<font color="#FFFFFF">The good news is that intrinsic properties are
already captured reasonably well,</font>

89
00:06:22,321 --> 00:06:25,494
<font color="#FFFFFF">which points toward the hardest remaining challenges.</font>

90
00:06:25,574 --> 00:06:31,878
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors argue the most promising path forward
is to enrich these models with structural information,</font>

91
00:06:31,958 --> 00:06:34,456
<font color="#FFFFFF">antigen context, and physics-based priors,</font>

92
00:06:34,536 --> 00:06:38,200
<font color="#FFFFFF">and to keep growing open antibody fitness datasets
like FLAb.</font>
